# Summary
Introduced a control-plane availability bug. The epoch stored for active_metadata_proof does not reliably represent the epoch in which that proof was actually produced/observed, but the new floor check treats it as authoritative and rejects otherwise valid epoch-local command-log progress.
The new active_metadata_proof_epoch is used to decide whether an active primary's observed metadata proof may be accepted when the metadata command log index is not monotonically comparable to the stored floor. However, complete_pg_peering and complete_ready_pg_peerings set active_metadata_proof_epoch after bump_epoch(), even though active_metadata_proof was obtained from peering observations in the previous cluster epoch. Additionally, heartbeat handling updates active_metadata_proof_epoch whenever the proof tuple is equal, but the proof tuple itself does not carry the metadata command epoch; an equal proof reported in a later cluster epoch may still represent the prior epoch's state. The validator then requires active_floor_epoch < observed_epoch before accepting epoch-local progress with a reset/lower log index. As a result, after activation or after an epoch bump followed by an equal-proof heartbeat, the first legitimate metadata command in the current epoch can produce a lower-index proof that is rejected as PgActiveMetadataProofMismatch. Rejected heartbeats can prevent lease renewal and make the PG unavailable. An authenticated S3 client can trigger the failure by issuing metadata-changing operations after an administrative/recovery epoch transition where the stored floor has a higher prior-epoch log index.

# Resolution
Fixed by commit https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/14e0961f (`14e0961f Fix active metadata proof epoch stamping`).

The fix records active metadata proof provenance using the epoch in which the proof was actually observed. `complete_pg_peering` and `complete_ready_pg_peerings` now capture the peering epoch before the activation epoch bump and store that as `active_metadata_proof_epoch`. Equal-proof active heartbeats no longer restamp the proof epoch, because the proof tuple does not itself identify the metadata command epoch. Only real metadata proof progress updates the stored proof and epoch.

The commit adds regressions for direct peering completion, batch peering completion, equal-proof heartbeat preservation, and accepting the first legitimate current-epoch lower-index metadata progress after activation.

