## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC against the checked-out commit. The relevant code matches the suspected bug: complete_pg_peering collects active_metadata_proof before bump_epoch(), then stores active_metadata_proof_epoch as the post-bump active epoch at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:2177-2211; complete_ready_pg_peerings has the same pattern at 2267-2291; heartbeat validation calls metadata_proof_satisfies_active_primary_observation_floor and returns PgActiveMetadataProofMismatch at 5637-5644; the floor helper only permits non-monotonic epoch-local progress when active_floor_epoch < observed_epoch at 5776-5792. I added a failing PoC test, poc_expected_current_epoch_progress_is_rejected, that uses the real SingleAuthorityControlPlane flow: bootstrap a PG, heartbeat a Peering proof with index 42 in ClusterEpoch(3), complete peering which bumps to ClusterEpoch(4), observe that active_metadata_proof_epoch is incorrectly stamped Some(ClusterEpoch(4)), then send an Active heartbeat in epoch 4 with a legitimate lower epoch-local proof index 1. The PoC prints that the same lower proof would be accepted if the proof epoch were the true peering epoch, but is rejected with the stored post-bump epoch. Command output: peering_epoch=ClusterEpoch(3) active_epoch=ClusterEpoch(4) stamped_epoch=Some(ClusterEpoch(4)); with true proof epoch ClusterEpoch(3), lower proof accepted by floor check: true; with stored proof epoch Some(ClusterEpoch(4)), lower proof accepted by floor check: false; then the test panics because heartbeat returned PgActiveMetadataProofMismatch { pg_id: 7, node_id: 1, cluster_epoch: ClusterEpoch(4), expected: PgMetadataProof { applied_log_index: 42, applied_log_hash: 2748, state_digest: 3567 }, actual: PgMetadataProof { applied_log_index: 1, applied_log_hash: 291, state_digest: 1110 } }. This reproduces the control-plane availability failure: the heartbeat carrying current-epoch metadata progress is rejected instead of renewing. I also